---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const slug = "kobo-storygraph-integration-2026";
const title = "Kobo StoryGraph Integration 2026: Track Your EPUB Reading";
const description = "Kobo and StoryGraph integration is live. Sync your Kobo EPUB reading to StoryGraph to track books, set goals, and get personalized recs. Full setup guide.";
const pubDate = "2026-07-02";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalUrl={`https://converter-epub.com/blog/${slug}/`} ogType="article" datePublished={pubDate}>
  <main class="blog-post">
    <article itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article">
      <meta itemprop="headline" content={title} />
      <meta itemprop="description" content={description} />
      <meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-07-02" />
      <span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Person">
        <span itemprop="name">Jason Mai</span>
      </span>

      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-07-02">Published July 2, 2026</time></p>

      <div set:html={`
<p>On Monday, Rakuten Kobo announced a partnership with StoryGraph, the popular community-driven reading tracker, bringing native reading-log syncing to Kobo e-readers for the first time. If you read EPUB files on a Kobo — and millions do, since Kobo is the most popular EPUB-native e-reader on the market — this integration changes how you track your reading life.</p> <p>StoryGraph has grown rapidly since launching as an alternative to Goodreads. Its tag-based mood tracking, content-warning system, and half-star ratings appealed to readers who found Amazon's platform rigid and ad-heavy. But until now, StoryGraph users who read on a Kobo had to manually log every book they finished. The new integration closes that gap, and it makes Kobo an even stronger choice for EPUB readers who want a connected, data-rich reading experience.</p> <h2>What the Kobo-StoryGraph Integration Does</h2> <p>The integration works through Kobo's built-in Reading Life feature. When you finish a book on your Kobo e-reader or in the Kobo mobile app, your reading data — including the book title, author, reading duration, date finished, and page count — syncs automatically to your StoryGraph account.</p> <p>This means:</p> <ul> <li><strong>No more manual logging.</strong> Books you finish on Kobo appear in your StoryGraph reading history without any extra steps.</li> <li><strong>Reading stats are richer.</strong> StoryGraph uses Kobo's actual reading-time data (not just an estimated finish date) to build your reading pace profile.</li> <li><strong>Your existing StoryGraph data stays intact.</strong> If you have been manually logging books in StoryGraph for years, your history is not overwritten. Only new Kobo finishes sync forward.</li> <li><strong>Books from any source work.</strong> EPUB files sideloaded through Adobe Digital Editions, purchased from Google Play Books, or borrowed from a library via OverDrive all sync to StoryGraph as long as they are opened and finished on a Kobo device.</li> </ul> <h2>Why This Matters for EPUB Readers</h2> <p>The EPUB ecosystem has always been fragmented when it comes to reading-data portability. Kindle users get Amazon's Reading Insights dashboard, which tracks pages turned and streaks. But Kindle's data stays inside Amazon's ecosystem — you cannot export it, and you lose access if you switch to a non-Amazon device.</p> <p>Kobo's integration with StoryGraph represents a different philosophy. StoryGraph is platform-agnostic. You can log books from any source — Kobo, Kindle, physical books, library borrows — and see them in one dashboard. The Kobo integration simply makes the Kobo-to-StoryGraph path automatic, which removes the biggest friction point for EPUB readers who want to track their reading.</p> <p>For EPUB-first readers, this is the best of both worlds: a device that treats EPUB files as first-class citizens, connected to a tracking platform that does not lock you into a single hardware vendor.</p> <h2>How to Set Up StoryGraph on Your Kobo</h2> <p>The setup process takes about two minutes and requires a Kobo e-reader running the latest firmware (5.19 or newer) and an existing StoryGraph account.</p> <ol> <li><strong>Update your Kobo firmware.</strong> Go to Settings > Device Information > Software Version. If you are not on version 5.19 or later, connect to Wi-Fi and check for updates under Settings > Device Information > Update.</li> <li><strong>Create or log in to StoryGraph.</strong> If you do not have a StoryGraph account, visit <a href="https://thestorygraph.com" rel="noopener" target="_blank">thestorygraph.com</a> and sign up. A free account gives you unlimited book tracking, reading stats, and personalized recommendations. A StoryGraph Plus subscription ($5.99/month or $54/year) adds expanded reading challenges and deeper analytics.</li> <li><strong>Enable the Kobo integration in StoryGraph.</strong> In the StoryGraph web app, go to Settings > Connected Accounts > Connect to Kobo. You will be prompted to authorize StoryGraph to access your Kobo reading data. This uses Kobo's official API — no third-party plugins or workarounds are involved.</li> <li><strong>Confirm the connection on your Kobo.</strong> On your Kobo e-reader, go to Settings > Accounts > Social and confirm that StoryGraph linking is enabled. You should see a "Connected to StoryGraph" status indicator.</li> <li><strong>Finish a book to test the sync.</strong> Open an EPUB on your Kobo and flip to the last page. When you close the book marked as finished, your Kobo syncs the data to StoryGraph. Check your StoryGraph "Read" shelf — the book should appear within 30 seconds.</li> </ol> <h2>Reading Features StoryGraph Offers Kobo Users</h2> <p>Once the integration is active, Kobo readers gain access to StoryGraph's full feature set without leaving their reading flow:</p> <table> <tr><th>Feature</th><th>What It Does for Kobo Readers</th></tr> <tr><td>Auto-log finished books</td><td>Books finished on your Kobo appear instantly on your StoryGraph "Read" shelf</td></tr> <tr><td>Reading pace tracking</td><td>StoryGraph calculates your pages-per-day and average reading speed from Kobo's timing data</td></tr> <tr><td>Reading challenges</td><td>Set annual or custom book-count goals; progress updates automatically as you finish books on Kobo</td></tr> <tr><td>Mood and pace filters</td><td>Discover new EPUBs by matching your preferred reading mood (funny, dark, emotional) and pace (slow, medium, fast)</td></tr> <tr><td>Content warnings</td><td>Community-curated content tags help you avoid unexpected themes without spoilers</td></tr> <tr><td>Buddy reads</td><td>Invite friends to read the same EPUB on a shared schedule; discussion prompts appear chapter-by-chapter</td></tr> <tr><td>Reading statistics dashboard</td><td>See your monthly reading volume, genre breakdown, author diversity, and reading streaks in one view</td></tr> </table> <h2>How It Compares to Kindle Reading Insights</h2> <p>Amazon's Reading Insights, available on Kindle devices and the Kindle app, tracks pages turned and daily reading streaks. It is a solid feature for Kindle-only readers, but it has significant limitations that the Kobo-StoryGraph combination solves:</p> <table> <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Kindle Reading Insights</th><th>Kobo + StoryGraph</th></tr> <tr><td>Data portability</td><td>Locked to Amazon — no export, no API</td><td>StoryGraph data is accessible via web and API</td></tr> <tr><td>Multi-device support</td><td>Kindle devices and Kindle app only</td><td>Kobo + any other device or physical books manually logged</td></tr> <tr><td>Mood tracking</td><td>Not available</td><td>Full mood and pace tagging for every book</td></tr> <tr><td>Content warnings</td><td>Not available</td><td>Community-driven content warning system</td></tr> <tr><td>Reading pace</td><td>Streak-based (days read)</td><td>Pages-per-day, reading speed, pace profile</td></tr> <tr><td>Personalized recommendations</td><td>Based on purchase history</td><td>Based on mood, pace, genre, and your actual reading data</td></tr> <tr><td>Import from other platforms</td><td>Not possible</td><td>Import from Goodreads, CSV, or manual entry</td></tr> </table> <p>The comparison is not about which platform has more features — it is about who owns your reading data. With Kindle, your reading history is locked to Amazon. With Kobo and StoryGraph, your reading data follows you regardless of which device or app you use next.</p> <h2>What This Means for the EPUB Ecosystem</h2> <p>The Kobo-StoryGraph integration is a small change technically — a single API hook — but it signals something larger about the EPUB ecosystem's direction. Kobo is investing in making the EPUB reading experience more connected, more social, and more data-rich, without locking users into a proprietary format or platform.</p> <p>Consider the contrast with Amazon's approach. When Amazon introduced Reading Insights in 2022, it gave Kindle users useful data but kept that data entirely within Amazon's ecosystem. If you switch from Kindle to Kobo tomorrow, your five years of Reading Insights data stays behind. The Kobo-StoryGraph model is the opposite: your reading history lives in an independent platform that works with any book format from any source.</p> <p>For readers who care about EPUB — the open, standards-based format that works on every device worth owning — this is the kind of infrastructure investment that makes the ecosystem stronger. A thriving EPUB reading ecosystem needs not just good devices and good file formats, but also good tools for managing the reading experience. StoryGraph is one of those tools, and its Kobo integration makes it meaningfully better for EPUB readers.</p> <h2>Limitations to Know Before You Sync</h2> <p>The integration is live as of early July 2026, but there are a few limitations worth understanding:</p> <ul> <li><strong>One-way sync only.</strong> Books finished on Kobo sync to StoryGraph, but StoryGraph reading goals and To-Read lists do not sync back to your Kobo device. This may be added in a future update.</li> <li><strong>Requires Wi-Fi sync.</strong> Your Kobo needs an active Wi-Fi connection to push reading data to StoryGraph. If you read in airplane mode, the sync happens the next time your device connects.</li> <li><strong>Sideloaded EPUB files may have gaps.</strong> EPUB files transferred via USB or Adobe Digital Editions sync correctly for most users, but some testers reported that books with non-standard metadata (missing ISBN or ASIN) do not always trigger the auto-log. A manual log in StoryGraph is always available as a fallback.</li> <li><strong>No partial-progress sync.</strong> StoryGraph only logs books you mark as finished on your Kobo. Your current reading progress (page 142 of 320) does not sync in real time. If you want to share what you are currently reading, you can manually set your "Currently Reading" status in StoryGraph.</li> <li><strong>Kobo app sync works differently.</strong> The integration works on both Kobo e-readers and the Kobo mobile app (iOS and Android), but the timing is slightly different. Mobile app finishes sync within a few seconds; e-reader finishes sync during the next Wi-Fi check.</li> </ul> <h2>How to Get the Most Out of Kobo + StoryGraph</h2> <p>Once the integration is active, here are a few ways Kobo EPUB readers can make the most of the combined setup:</p> <p><strong>Use StoryGraph's recommendation engine to find your next EPUB.</strong> StoryGraph's recommendation algorithm uses mood, pace, genre, and your past reading history to suggest books. Since your Kobo-finish data feeds into this, the recommendations improve the more you read on your Kobo. This is a real advantage over Amazon's purchase-history-based suggestions, which tend to recommend only bestsellers and series you have already bought into.</p> <p><strong>Set up reading challenges that sync automatically.</strong> StoryGraph supports annual book-count challenges and custom challenges (for example, "Read 12 non-fiction books this year"). With the Kobo integration, every book you finish on your Kobo automatically counts toward your challenge progress — no manual tracking needed.</p> <p><strong>Use the StoryGraph Plus reading pace data.</strong> StoryGraph Plus subscribers get access to expanded reading pace analytics, including how your reading speed varies by genre, author, and format. Pair this with the Kobo tracking data to identify which types of EPUBs you read fastest, and use that insight to plan your reading queue.</p> <p><strong>Keep manual logging muscle memory for non-Kobo reading.</strong> The integration only covers books finished on Kobo devices and the Kobo app. If you also read physical books, listen to audiobooks, or occasionally read on a Kindle, you will still need to log those manually in StoryGraph. The web interface and mobile app make this quick, but it is worth maintaining the habit.</p> <h2>FAQ</h2> <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does the Kobo-StoryGraph integration work with EPUB files from the library (OverDrive)?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. Library books borrowed through Kobo's built-in OverDrive integration count as Kobo books. When you finish a library EPUB on your Kobo, it syncs to StoryGraph the same way a purchased book does. Your StoryGraph reading data is yours regardless of how you acquired the file.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Will syncing to StoryGraph drain my Kobo battery faster?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>No. The sync only triggers when you finish a book (not continuously), and transmission happens during the next regular Wi-Fi check. The impact on battery life is negligible — comparable to a single store sync after a purchase.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I connect multiple Kobo devices to the same StoryGraph account?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. StoryGraph treats your Kobo account as the connection point, not an individual device. If you have a Kobo Libra Colour for home use and a Kobo Clara BW for travel, books finished on either device sync to the same StoryGraph profile.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">What happens to my existing StoryGraph data when I connect Kobo?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Nothing changes. Your existing reading history, shelves, and reviews remain untouched. The integration only adds new books as you finish them on your Kobo going forward. There is no import of past reading data from Kobo, so any books you finished before connecting must stay as manually logged entries.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does this work with KEPUB files or only standard EPUB?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Both. Kobo's native KEPUB format and standard EPUB files both trigger the StoryGraph sync when marked as finished. If you use Calibre to convert EPUBs to KEPUB before sideloading, the integration works exactly the same way — the finishing event is handled at the Kobo OS level, not the file-format level.</p> </div> </div>
      `}/>
    </article>
  </main>
